      {
        "id": 38148,
        "name": "Rabit",
        "username": "Rabit",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/rabit/{size}/7330_2.png",
        "created_at": "2012-09-10T16:14:00.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"arenson9\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/arenson9/48/1900_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> arenson9:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>One way to think of this, perhaps, is that all forty cards start in the deck, but during play some cards leave the deck to go into hand, into play, or into the hero's trash.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>I think this is the key - it's not that there are different definitions, it's that the state changes depending on if you're in game or not.</p>\n<p>Magic avoided this by <a href=\"http://gatherer.wizards.com/Pages/Search/Default.aspx?text=+%5B%22search%20your%20library%22%5D\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\">using \"library\" to represent the deck during play</a>, but I personally&nbsp;don't feel SotM needs to go to that level, as most other games haven't, either. That's just me, though.  <img src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/images/emoji/apple/slightly_smiling_face.png?v=12\" title=\":slightly_smiling_face:\" class=\"emoji\" alt=\":slightly_smiling_face:\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\"></p>",
